+## Preserving Forward-Referenced Flags
+
+When a view model owns a flag that no current code path actually flips (a dead flag), the instinct is to delete it. Resist this when the flag is about to be wired up in an upcoming story — deleting it turns a one-line wiring change into a multi-file refactor.
+
+Instead, keep the flag declared, default it to `false`, and document its forward reference in a doc comment. The comment should name the wiring point so the next person (or a future grep) can find it instantly.
+
+```swift
+// FlipbookViewModel.swift — S11-14 (Wire loading skeletons) landed, but this VM
+// still seeds cards synchronously from `Card.samples`; `isLoading` stays false
+// until S12-XX swaps in the real LessonRepository.fetch(for:) call.
+@MainActor
+public final class FlipbookViewModel: ObservableObject {
+    /// Set `true` while an async card fetch is in flight. Currently always `false`
+    /// because cards are seeded synchronously from in-memory samples; flips on
+    /// once `LessonRepository.fetch(for:)` replaces the stub (planned S12-XX).
+    @Published public private(set) var isLoading = false
+}
+```
+
+The doc comment must answer two questions: **why it's currently dead** (what's stubbed) and **what will light it up** (story/PR that replaces the stub). Without that, a future reader correctly assumes it's unused and removes it. This pattern is preferable to both (a) deleting and re-adding later — the re-add is now scattered across VM + view + tests — and (b) adding an inline `// TODO: wire this up` without context, which decays into noise.
